# 32. Real-World Examples

## Example 1: E-Commerce Website

An e-commerce frontend runs with three Nginx Pods.

```text
           Customer
               |
        External Entry Point
               |
          ClusterIP Service
               |
      ---------------------
      |         |         |
    Nginx-1   Nginx-2   Nginx-3
```

A Deployment manages the Pods.

A normal Service distributes traffic among eligible Pods.

This is a common stateless application architecture.

## Example 2: MySQL Database Cluster

```text
          Backend Application
                  |
          Headless Service
                  |
      -------------------------
      |           |           |
   mysql-0      mysql-1     mysql-2
   Primary      Replica     Replica
      |           |           |
     PVC-0       PVC-1       PVC-2
```

A StatefulSet manages stable Pod identities.

A Headless Service provides DNS discovery.

PersistentVolumeClaims provide storage.

Database replication must be configured separately.

## Example 3: Kafka Cluster

Kafka brokers may require predictable identities and direct communication with other brokers.

```text
          Kafka Clients
                |
        Broker Discovery
                |
      ---------------------
      |         |         |
   broker-0  broker-1  broker-2
```

StatefulSets and Headless Services can support stable broker identities and discovery.

Kafka itself manages broker metadata, partition replication, and other cluster-level behavior.

